There are two logic bugs:

1. `if data[i] == max_val` compares the current element to the **best value so far**, not to the previous element in the current streak. For `[2, 2, 1, 1, 1]`, after seeing the first two `2`s, `max_val` is still `2`, so the later run of `1`s is never accumulated correctly.

2. The final streak is never compared against `max_count`. Your expected answer `(1, 3)` is the streak at the end of the input, so it is lost before the function returns.

<solution language="rust" verdict="fix">
fn longest_streak(data: &Vec<i32>) -> (i32, usize) {
    let mut max_val = data[0];
    let mut max_count: usize = 1;
    let mut current_count: usize = 1;

    for i in 1..data.len() {
        if data[i] == data[i - 1] {
            current_count += 1;
        } else if current_count > max_count {
            max_count = current_count;
            max_val = data[i - 1];
            current_count = 1;
        } else {
            current_count = 1;
        }
    }
    if current_count > max_count {
        max_count = current_count;
        max_val = data[data.len() - 1];
    }
    (max_val, max_count)
}
</solution>

