message.content:
The bug is in `Increment()`:

```go
c.count++
```

`c.count++` is not atomic. It performs a read, increment, and write. When many goroutines run concurrently, two goroutines can read the same value at the same time, both write back the same incremented value, and one increment is lost. That’s why the final count can be lower than `1000` under heavy load.

`wg.Wait()` only ensures all goroutines finished before printing; it does not prevent data races.

Fix: protect `count` with a mutex.

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

type Counter struct {
    mu    sync.Mutex
    count int
}

func (c *Counter) Increment() {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()
    c.count++
}

func (c *Counter) GetCount() int {
    c.mu.Lock()
    defer c.mu.Unlock()
    return c.count
}

func main() {
    counter := &Counter{}
    var wg sync.WaitGroup

    for i := 0; i < 1000; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func() {
            defer wg.Done()
            counter.Increment()
        }()
    }

    wg.Wait()
    fmt.Println("Final count:", counter.GetCount())
}
</solution>

